>but this would lead to problems after nanotech.  When owls can be
>transformed into almost superhuman hyperbeings with the augmentation of
>intelligence intensifier interfaces, they'd have the potential for reason.  

This classification is fine with me, but I don't see the "problem" with the
possibility of technological augmentation of beasts into rational animals.
Beasts unto themselves still won't have the potential for reason, beasts
augmented with technology will.  Then we'll have two options: distinguish
between beasts and what David Brin calls "uplifted" beasts, if there's any
easy way to tell the difference between the two, or we'll have to reclassify
all beasts into the category "presumed rational," and treat ones we can
prove haven't been uplifted as beasts.

Some animal rights advocates claim that man has an interest in keeping
beasts down in order to exploit them, but what they don't realize is that we
stand to benefit far more from them if they are uplifted so that we can
extend the sphere of production, trade, and the division of labor to them
that we could ever get out of them as mere dumb beasts.  The extropian atti-
tude towards uplifting beasts is one of hope, not fear.  Of course, most of
those animal rights advocates are anti-capitalists, so they wouldn't like
it if their precious beasts became entrepreneurs...

>If we're sure that (natural) rights = negative rights = (ancient)
>liberties, then the rights are enforced and the liberties acquired / won
>/ gained.

These all imply a state of nature in which men don't have these liberties,
which isn't/wasn't necessarily the case.

>I prefer freedoms or liberties...

Liberties is pretty good, but freedom can easily be construed as "freedom
from want, fear, etc.," as in Roosevelt II's "Four Freedoms" speech.

The classical distinction between a civil right and a civil liberty is
that a civil liberty was something that had traditionally not been
violated, whereas a civil right was something that had been violated and
had to be enforced, usually against the State.

