His case wasn't against all philosophers up to his time, it was against
representationalists, like Descartes and Locke.  Against them, he succeeded
in proving that you can't deduce anything from sense-impressions with
certainty, because the reliability of sense-impressions can't be established
within the representationalist view.  Berkeley anticipated and influenced
him some in the skeptical critique of representationalism.

They didn't address realism, because it had fallen out of fashion.

>What's 
>exciting about evolutionary epistemology and PCR is that it's the first 
>philosophical explanation of the *fact* of human knowledge that actually 
>addresses the problem that Hume pointed out, rather than ignoring it and 
