Subject: [#94-3-194] SCI: Inflation (was: PCR: Causality (Creation ...

Mike Price forwards the interesting *New Scientist* article:
>According to a popular but very speculative theory, first proposed by
>the American physicist Alan Guth, when the Universe was about 3 x 10^-44
>seconds it began to expand exponentially fast.  This so-called
>_inflationary epoch_ ended at about 3 x 10^-35 seconds.  [...]
>
>So violent was inflation that some have likened the big bang to a hand
>grenade going off in the middle of a nuclear explosion.  During
>inflation, space blew up, or "inflated", until it was 10^50 time bigger
>than it had been.

One thing about inflation that I haven't seen described or explained, is
just what it is that space inflates relative to.  (And "to what it had
been" is no answer!)  If someone claimed that time was about to slow down,
stop, run backwards, and return to normal without explaining what this was
relative to, this would be a ridiculously non-operational concept.  Our
consciousness, clocks, etc simply operate at the local rate of time, and
the thermodynamic arrow dictates the direction of time.  If all of space
was equally expanding, any measuring rods, observers' heads, etc, would be
expanding in exactly the same proportion; hence would not notice anything.
(Rather brave, versatile observers!)

When the universe is expanding, keeping the Planck length fixed at around
10^-35 m. means that the number of spacetime particles or lumps of
spacetime foam that fit into the local bubble's light cone will increase.
If inflation just means that the number of these spacetime lumps increases
exponentially then that would do.  If the spacetime lumps kept inflating
themselves, no one would ever notice.

