Subject: [#94-3-195] Natural Rights and Ancient Liberties

> So victims could sue for damages incurred whilst on public property, and 
> would also be expected to sue if neighborhood effects damaged their private
> property.  It looks as if people could have the right to damage public
> property e.g. beaches, provided no one was specifically harmed.

The most pleasant beaches south of where I live are privately owned and 
operated. One must pay a small fee for admittance, a slightly larger fee 
for overnight camping, but this is the case with the most popular 
state-owned beaches also.

"Public property" is an oxymoron and a contradiction in terms. "Public 
property" really means the private property which is owned by the 
organization called the state, that is, by the group of people who have 
power and influence in the government, at any level.

Any owner of private property may choose to allow public access to that
property, and in fact there are strong economic incentives for some to do
so. The original and true "public space" is the marketplace, the agora,
and there is no need for the fiction of "public property" to ensure this.
The state, like any other owner of private property, chooses to allow
public access to some of its properties and to restrict access to other of
its properties, and like any other owner of private property it makes
these decisions based on its own interests. That some of these interests
are political is irrelevant to whether the property is private (as all
property must be) or "owned by the public" as the conventional fiction has
it. 

As Tim Starr correctly points out, the solution to the problems created 
by this contradiction in terms is to maximally privatize the property of 
the state. But in the meantime, we can get a big conceptual payoff by 
recognizing that the state is just a huge conglomerate, and that "public 
property" is actually the private property belonging to it and its 
various subsidiaries.

If we understand "private" to mean "belonging to an identifiable proper
subset of people", then it is surely true that what is not private is not
property. The issue of whether or not public access is permitted by the
owner is a separate one. I cannot unrestrictedly traipse through the
"publicly owned" Lawrence Livermore Labs, but I can walk into any of a
number of privately owned shops with very few restrictions at all. 

