From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Mar 1994 18:03:12 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-198] PCR: Is Certainty Relevant?

In messages #94-3-143 and #94-3-144 (remailed on March 12) Tim Starr
and Reilly Jones have both asked questions about my operational
definition of the word "certain".

Reilly is using a definition (repeatedly citing his dictionary as his
authority) which explicitly denies the point I was trying to make. No
such thing as certainty beyond the shadow of a doubt exists, although
it must comfort some people to think it does. In many useful cases,
doubt can be reduced to a level beyond the number of significant
digits we are working with, which makes it effectively zero. But there
is always the shadow of a doubt. Physical scientists are thoroughly
familiar with non-zero quantities that are too small to measure
directly.

Tim asks for a genus and a differentia for certainty. Certainty is an
attitudinal proposition. Certainty is an attitude that we have with
regard to beliefs/ideas/propositions. That is its genus, if you will.
Its differentia is that it is the attitude we have toward propositions
that causes us to act as if those propositions were true. This is what
we mean when we say "I am certain that x". We mean "I intend to act as
if x were true".

Now, clearly, we can have different degrees of confidence in the truth
of a matter. This is the idea I was trying to get across with my tired
example of my nose vis-a-vis Deng Xiaoping. Given the analytical
definition above, it seems clear that there can be different degrees
of certainty about a matter, or in other words, that certainty is
relative.

What does all this have to do with PanCritical Rationalism? PCR says
that we do not ultimately base our knowledge on justifying ideas,
because ultimately we have to come to some fundamental axiomatic
idea(s) which we can only defend by appeals to faith or intuition.
This is the Skeptic's problem. Instead, PCR suggests that our
knowledge evolves through a process of blind variation and non-blind
selection.  The selective process is criticism. One of the many
varieties of criticism is the check for logical consistency, which is
the process of comparing a pair of ideas to see whether they
contradict one another. The outcome of criticism is the rejection of
the member of the pair of which we are less certain. In order to do
this, we have to admit that we can indeed hold different degrees of
certainty about different ideas.

Now it may seem that at least the law of noncontradiction may have
special status in this scheme. But PCR insists that we hold the law of
noncontradiction itself up to critical comparisons. We are safe in
doing this because the law of noncontradiction always wins; it is in
fact difficult or impossible to successfully criticize. (Side note: I
haven't read anything about fuzzy logic yet.) The reason for this is
that there are more criticisms than just the criticism of logical
consistency (which is itself based on the law of noncontradiction,
among others). Another valid criticism of an idea, ceteris paribus, is
its usefulness and applicability. While a little slipperier, we do
actually use this criticism all the time, and the alternatives to the
law of noncontradiction always flunk this one. A good exercise for the
reader is to try to think of and explicitly state reasons why a given
alternative to the law of noncontradiction is worse than the law of
noncontradiction. Each of these is a potential criticism.

There are still some testy issues at the foundations of PCR. It is my
opinion that the foundation of PCR is considerably sturdier than the
foundations of traditional justificationist philosophies (which are
vulnerable to the attacks of the classical Skeptics and of Hume) and
also sturdier than the foundation of at least Ayn Rand's version of
objectivism. I am not familiar with Kelley's. Perhaps if Tim could
succinctly state the points of divergence between the two (that is,
where does Kelley explicitly *disagree* with Rand?) I might be able to
address this.

Eric Watt Forste          finger arkuat@netcom.com for the rest of my .sig




Footnote: Reilly Jones accuses me of "pure authority worship" for not
being impressed by an extended quote from Mussolini. My response: huh?
If Mussolini has an idea that is relevant to the discussion at hand,
then surely a paraphrase of the idea would make its relevance to the
discussion more clear, since Mussolini has not been a party to the
discussion. Giving credit where credit is due does not require the use
of extended quotations from non-list-members.

Footnote: Reilly also claims that certainty is possible under
relativism (as he and Tim use the term) because relativism denies the
existence of objective reality. Again: huh? Most of the ensuing
discussion is based on the distinction between "objective reality" and
"subjective reality". This distinction is spurious. My subjective
perceptions and states of mind are physical events in my brain; they
are elements of objective reality as surely as solar fusion or plant
growth is. My question: if relativism denies the existence of
"objective reality" then what is left to be certain of? Ones
intentions? Ones intentions about what? Ones intentions about ones
subjective states of mind? This line of inquiry loses its interest
rapidly. Reality is reality.




