From: LEVY%LENNY@venus.cis.yale.edu
Date: Wed, 16 Mar 1994 01:02:34 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-3-202] LANG: Evolution of Language

My apologies to Steve Whitrow for misunderstanding his major point, which
seems to be that words, like phenotypes, can evolve to fill a niche.  This
makes a good deal of sense to me, and (to concede Nan W.'s point as well)
provides a sense in which language can evolve.

My main reason for opposing the view that language evolves comes from looking
at the things that interest *me* as a (former) linguist, namely, grammatical
and phonological patterns, as opposed to words.  When people use the term
"linguistics" (in academia, at least), it is the study of grammar and sound
that they are most often referring to.  For these sorts of systems, it is
much harder to find support for evolution: what, for example, would be the
"niche" being filled by a language changing from SOV to SVO word order,
as German is doing?

Of course, just because modern linguists don't focus on word meanings all
that much doesn't mean that word meanings (and their evolution) aren't
an interesting part of *language*.  So, to repeat myself, I think it makes
sense to say that languages evolve to fill a niche, as long as you keep
the focus on word meaning, and not formal structure.

-- Simon!

