> extropy outputs of some of the greatest like Einstein, it seems to
> me that the brain's "maximum extropy density" is higher than markets.  
> The human brain has been described as the most complex object on the
> planet, although it seems fair to say the other spontaneous orders such
> as markets are probably underrated due to their abstract nature.

   My point is that humans are often more productive when they can
coordinate efforts. For example, Einstein didn't invent the theory of
relativity by himself. He took ideas from many other physicists, and his
wife (a mathematician) supposedly did a lot of the mathematics required.
The whole reason there is a scientific community is because the more
people that receive your memes, the higher probability that one of them
will make a favorable mutation to it. And how much of Einstein's
"extropy" is owed to Newton, Planck, and Lorentz?
  This is not to downplay Einstien's extraordinary insight (a better
example of great individual achieivement would be Newton, Gauss, or
Archimedes) but to point out that humans can increase extropy by
networking. There is also the argument of Tim May that the early 
pioneers in science got the "easy picking's" in the search space and
now discoveries are alot harder. If that's true, a discovery credited
to a group of physicists in modern times should be corrected for 
its difficultly.

> >   But this is only your feeling, you have no proof. A reductionist
> >would note that one electron is as good as any other and everyday
> >millions of atoms in your body are replaced.
> 
> I agree that one particle is as good as another -- they're not conscious
> or anything -- but maybe simulating the position of particles is not the
> same as actually having the particles assembled together in the proper
> places.  If I was accurately copied, and the original destroyed, I think
> it would be reasonable to assume I'd survive.  But would you be entirely
> happy if the positions of all your particles were recorded, the original
> rjc got destroyed, the particles were then simulated on a machine for ten
> years, then a new rjc was assembled from the computed positions at the
> end of that time?

  Ok, think of the system of particles that makes up the brain as a
blackbox.

                     +-----------------+
Sensory Input ---->  | brain           | ----> output (move an arm, etc)
                     +-----------------+

  Now, if there exists a machine which can simulate the particles in
the box perfectly and its predictions are never wrong, then we can replace
the box with a simulation. Given the same input, the machine will always
produce the same output as a real brain would. As far as everything
that matters goes in the real world, doing a job, conversing, lifting
boxes, eating, this box will be identical. If for some reason, there is
a "ghost" in the machine which causes the simulation to diverge from 
the output of the "real" box of particles, then the simulation is not
perfect. At this point, either 1 of 2 things are true:
1) the effect of the ghost is minimal and there is a natural law which
governs it
2) the effect of the ghost has no pattern we can discover whatsoever
(in this case, it is totally random and can't be predicted)

   Now, this argument has no bearing on the question "is it you?" I have
such a strong survival instinct that my trust in science isn't large
enough to take the chance (nor would I hold on to a high tension line
even though basic physics tells us that as long as you don't ground yourself,
you won't be shocked) I opt for a much more conservative transition of
gradually upgrading and augmenting myself until my original brain becomes
a miniscule part of what I really am.

   From the theory of Turing Machines, we know that no matter what you
do to a turning machine, you can't seem to improve on how much it can
compute. What it would take to convince me that simulation won't work is
1) demonstrate that a more powerful machine can exist and can't be simulated
by ordinary computers (but then, why couldn't we build a new machine
based on these particles?)
2) show that quantum non-determinism plays a much larger effect in the brain
than molecular scale mechno-electro-chemical changes.
3) demonstrate a ghost

> >   Order (Extropy, negentropy) isn't only found in the brain however. There
> >are many other areas of nature which exibit high self-order and in the lab
> >"bunches of particles" can also be shown to create spontaneous orders.
> >Freezing water creates lots of order, so does coal-mines+pressure -> diamond.
> It would be some pretty cool ice cube that ever discovered penicillin or
> wrote a symphony...

   There are computers now which can write symphonies. There are also
programs which can search for and test new drugs without animals.

> One problem with reductionism is the "irreversibility paradox".  According
> to reductionism, everything can be reduced to the behaviour of particles.
> At the macrocosmic level there is clearly a thermodynamic arrow of time,
> but at the microcosmic level time is reversible, with no evidence of such
> an arrow.  Thus, the thermodynamic arrow does not reduce to the
> microcosmic level.

   It's no more a real paradox than the twin paradox or the
"General Relativity <-> Quantum mechanics" paradox. We simply don't
know how to derive the arrow of time via statistical mechanics. We can't
derive economic laws from QM either. There are some physicists who think
there is a parity violating process which will lead to the arrow of
time.  Personally, I think the arrow of time comes from the initial
conditions of the universe and basic probability. We don't see a time
reversal of a window breaking because of the improbability of all of the
pieces having the right speed and trajectory at the same time. Entropy
increases because it is more probable that particles have random
trajectories than all rushing to one side of a box. Consider Conway's
Life (in certain configurations) as a non-physical example.

> >   Patently false. First of all, a reductionist would claim that it is
> >the organization of the particles (the brain) that is important.
> 
> If the organization of the particles is important, doesn't this mean that
> they must be put into place, and "simulating" them on a machine only gives
> a simulation, not the true organization?

 No. What's important is what the organization of your particles do
in relation to other's organization of particles. If a virtual set
of particles perform the same actions as the real set, what's the difference?
For instance, if a box wired up to your vocal cords says "hello, how 
are you doing?" and your brain does the same thing, does it matter
that the particles were simulated?
  I maintain that there is no difference between reality and a perfect
simulation of it. If the whole universe were simulated, it would make
absolutely no difference.
 
> > Secondly, the
> >correct analogy is particle:individual::brain:society.
> 
> It's particle:individual::individual:groups of individuals/society
> if you like.  I don't see why you've got brain:society.

  Mistake, I made I think. I'm saying a particle is to a brain as
an individual is to a society. A brain is a group of particles. A
society is a group of individuals.

> >By claiming that
> >the brain as a whole is fundamentally important, you are making the same
> >mistake statists make in elevating the importance of the group above
> >its constituent parts.
> 
> The statists are wrong because their groups such as command economies and 
> government are more entropic than individuals; brains are not more
> entropic than a bunch of particles.

  I would certainly agree that a command economy or government is more
entropic , but for different reasons. I think the mind is a spontaneous
order of parts of the brain just like a market economy is a spontaneous
order of men. Command economies are entropic because they chose one
path specifically instead of letting all paths compete and thus the
command economy doesn't evolve, it stagnates and disorders itself
relative to the market economy.

> You pointed out how extropic markets are -- how would you measure that?
> When we had the discussion on the extropy of Xtianity vs Newtonian 
> physics, etc last year, Nick Szabo couldn't get anyone to produce a
> satisfactory empirical benchmark which showed the extropy of an idea.
 
  I think you could probably measure it by narrowing the measurement
down to something like complexity and efficiency of the idea. As an
individual, I certainly couldn't produce a Pentium by myself nor could
I produce a Delta Clipper. Designs are so complex now that division of
labor is a neccessity. 

> As some examples, a crowd of football hooligans, a secret police force,
> or a gang of drug-crazed muggers probably contains more entropy
> together than any of them in isolation.

   But they are certainly more ordered than a single mugger. A black
labor market of bankrobbers, drug dealers, or muggers would certainly
be more efficient at crime than individuals. Even "command" crime
syndicates are more efficient in terms of dollars stolen.

